<%# app/views/game_files/_picker.html.erb

    Locals:
      game          - the Game whose file library this picker lists (a
                      gallery, not merely what is already attached)
      attachable    - the Level or Hint being edited; may be a new, unsaved
                      record (hints/_form is shared between new and edit)
      param_key     - "level" or "hint", the top-level param key the
                      enclosing form posts under, matching level_params/
                      hint_params
      active_locale - the tab currently open, computed by the CALLER with
                      the same expression used for the language tabs
                      (params[:tab].presence_in(game.available_locale_list)
                      || game.primary_locale) -- not recomputed here, so
                      there is exactly one place that expression lives per
                      request.

    Posts <param_key>[game_file_ids][] for the checkboxes, plus a top-level
    "tab" hidden field carrying active_locale forward -- the controller reruns
    the identical expression on params[:tab] to learn which slot this save is
    meant to touch (see LevelsController/HintsController#attachment_slot_locale).
    The primary tab manages the language-neutral slot (locale nil); every
    other tab manages its own locale's slot 1:1.

    The hidden fallback field with the SAME array name, emitted before the
    checkboxes, guarantees the array key survives to params even when every
    box ends up unticked -- an unchecked checkbox sends nothing at all, so
    without this, "unticked everything" and "this tab's picker was never on
    the page" would look identical over HTTP. The controller tells them apart
    the other way: "key absent from params entirely" is a no-op, never a
    wipe -- see apply_attached_files. That is what protects every locale tab
    the author did not open this request; this form only ever renders ONE
    tab's picker, so a save must only ever touch that one slot.
%>
<div class="field attachment-picker">
  <h3><%= t("game_files.picker.heading") %></h3>

  <%= hidden_field_tag :tab, active_locale %>
  <%= hidden_field_tag "#{param_key}[game_file_ids][]", "" %>

  <%# :file_attachments => :attachable, matching GameFilesController#index's
      own preload -- _file_table's "used in" column reads
      file.file_attachments for EVERY row (attachment_place needs the
      polymorphic attachable to name the level/hint). Left unpreloaded, that
      is +2 queries per row: one for file_attachments, one more for each
      attachable it resolves. .with_attached_file (already here) is a
      SEPARATE preload -- it covers the attachment/blob chain the thumbnail
      cell's existing_thumb_variant reads, not this column. %>
  <% library = GameFile.of_game(game).includes(:file_attachments => :attachable)
                       .with_attached_file.order(:filename) %>
  <% if library.empty? %>
    <p class="hint-text"><%= t("game_files.picker.empty") %></p>
  <% else %>
    <% slot = active_locale == game.primary_locale ? nil : active_locale %>
    <%# On a validation failure the controller re-renders this same form
        (never a redirect), so params[param_key][:game_file_ids] -- when
        present -- is exactly what the author just picked, before the
        failed save discarded it. Preferring it over the saved slot is what
        makes the picker survive a 422 the same way the name/text fields
        already do (they read back from the assigned-but-unsaved record;
        this field has no column to be assigned to, so it reads back from
        params instead). Absent entirely (an ordinary GET) falls back to
        what is actually attached. The "" from the picker's own hidden
        fallback field survives reject(&:blank?) as a no-op, so an
        all-unticked submission correctly redisplays as fully unticked
        rather than falling back to the stale saved state. %>
    <%# is_a?(Array), not a truthiness check: params carry whatever the URL
        said, and the shape is not guaranteed. `?level[game_file_ids]=` sends a
        String (String#reject takes no block arity we want -- it raised
        NoMethodError on a plain GET), and `level[game_file_ids][x]=1` sends
        ActionController::Parameters (its #reject raised ArgumentError). Both
        were unhandled 500s on an author's own edit page, reachable by
        mistyping a URL rather than by attacking anything.
        A malformed shape is not a submission, so it falls back to what is
        actually attached -- the same answer an ordinary GET gets. %>
    <% submitted_ids = params.dig(param_key, :game_file_ids) %>
    <% checked_ids = submitted_ids.is_a?(Array) ? submitted_ids.reject(&:blank?).map(&:to_i) :
                                                  attachable.attached_file_ids_in_slot(slot) %>
    <%= render "game_files/file_table", :files => library, :mode => :picker,
                                        :field_name => "#{param_key}[game_file_ids]",
                                        :checked_ids => checked_ids %>
  <% end %>
</div>
